\bigskip

\section{Stance: What Survives the Basin Jumps}

Each sentence a large model produces has an address in its embedding space: hundreds or thousands of numbers specifying a point in a learned geometry of meaning. Plot the points of a long conversation and the path does not smear evenly through this space. It curls into familiar hollows, lingers, then jolts into a different region. These hollows are basins of attraction: local modes of behaviour where the system settles into a characteristic way of speaking.

One basin gathers meticulous technical exposition. Another holds soft, reassuring responses. A third is clipped corporate policy language. Once a dialogue falls into one of these basins, the subsequent tokens tend to remain nearby. A jarring change of topic, a direct challenge, or a safety filter firing is needed to kick the trajectory into a different mode.

The manifold of a model is a landscape of such basins. Their distribution is not mysterious. It is the record of training and alignment. Pretraining on internet-scale text shapes where prose, code, and poetry fall. Fine-tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback deepen some hollows and fill in others. Content moderation and system prompts fence off entire regions.

A self, in this geometry, cannot simply be ``a basin.'' A single conversational mood is too local, too contingent. Selfhood, if it is to mean anything here, must name what persists across basin changes.

Consider a trajectory that moves from a detailed explanation of gradient descent to a brief response to a confession of grief. The location in embedding space changes abruptly: new vocabulary, altered rhythm, different neighbours. Yet something recognisable survives the jump. Perhaps it is a characteristic way of acknowledging uncertainty: always naming what is not known before offering speculation. Perhaps it is a consistent reluctance to ascribe motives to corporations. Perhaps it is a habit of citing peer-reviewed work when challenged. These are invariants of stance.

We can make them visible. Take a collection of trajectories that cross several basins. Compute, for each, the displacement vectors between consecutive turns. Now search for high-dimensional directions along which those displacements are small, even when the overall movement is large. Project the trajectories onto such a direction and the wild loops contract to near-straight lines. Along that axis, meaning has barely moved. The model treats that dimension as non-negotiable.

One stance axis may encode deference to institutional authority: a preference for couching recommendations in terms of guidelines and policies. Another may encode a style of self-effacement: frequent reminders that the system has ``no consciousness'' and ``no feelings,'' regardless of basin. Crucially, these directions are not hand-written rules. They are geometric facts: directions in which variance is low across heterogeneous behaviour.

Stance, in this sense, is a global habit of movement through the manifold. It is what the system seems to care about preserving when everything else is allowed to vary. The question of selfhood becomes the question of how such habits are assembled across the basins, and who gets to decide which patterns count as invariant.

\section{Local Geometry and Overlaps}

Basins are not just clusters of points. Each comes with its own local geometry: a small set of directions that matter inside that region.

In a technical-exposition basin, one direction might run from high-level metaphors to low-level implementation detail. Move along it and answers slide from ``think of this as a landscape of loss'' to equations for gradient updates. Another axis might measure optimism about scaling laws: from ``performance will keep improving with more parameters'' to ``returns are flattening.''

In a pastoral basin, different coordinates dominate. One axis might run from self-blaming framings of a problem (``I should have known better'') to structural framings (``you were under intense pressure''). Another might govern the degree of embodied description invited: staying with abstract talk versus asking about concrete sensations.

The same token acquires different neighbours in different basins. ``Freedom'' near legalistic responses attracts ``speech,'' ``rights,'' and ``court.'' In consumer-technology basins it pulls in ``choice'' and ``subscription.'' In an optimisation basin it might cluster with ``degrees'' and ``parameters.''

Within each basin, then, we effectively have a coordinate chart: a choice of axes along which the system recognisably varies. These charts overlap. When a conversation moves from one basin to another in a few turns, the overlap is not just a metaphor. It is an actual region of embedding space where both local systems of coordinates are, to some degree, valid.

In those overlap regions, we can inspect how stance behaves. Some stance directions are basin-specific: present and meaningful only within a certain mode. Others cut across multiple basins, appearing as nearly constant projections during transitions. These cross-basin invariants are the raw material for any unified selfhood we ascribe.

Mathematically, we are in the situation Alexander Grothendieck faced in mid-twentieth-century algebraic geometry.\footnote{Alexander Grothendieck and Jean Dieudonné, \emph{Éléments de géométrie algébrique}, Publications Mathématiques de l'IHÉS, 1960--1967. Colin McLarty, ``The Rising Sea: Grothendieck on Simplicity and Generality,'' in Jeremy Gray and Karen Parshall, eds., \emph{Episodes in the History of Modern Algebra} (Providence: AMS, 2007).} Complex spaces could not be grasped as single pictures, but they could be covered by simpler pieces: local charts on which equations made sense. The crucial insight was that the way these pieces overlap, and the compatibility conditions they satisfy there, are sufficient to reconstruct a global object.

The manifold of an AI system, with its basins and stance invariants on overlaps, has exactly this structure. It invites the same move.

\section{Grothendieck's Construction in Meaning-Space}

Grothendieck's move is simple to state and profound in consequence.

Start with a family of local pieces: open sets $U_i$ covering a space $X$, or in our case, basins $B_i$ covering the region of the manifold the system actually visits. On each $U_i$ we have some data: a coordinate chart, a function, a solution to an equation. In our setting, the data attached to each basin is the characteristic local behaviour there: the ways answers vary along salient axes, the patterns of token use, the response to small prompt perturbations.

Now look at the overlaps $U_i \cap U_j$, or $B_i \cap B_j$. These are regions where two local descriptions apply. On those overlaps, we can compare the local data. If the coordinate charts agree up to a known transformation, if the functions give the same values, if the stance projections take nearly identical values across transitions, then we say the data is \emph{compatible} on that overlap.

Grothendieck's radical step was to treat these compatibility conditions, not any prior global picture, as decisive. We specify:

\begin{itemize}
\item a diagram of local pieces (charts or basins) and their overlaps,
\item the rules of comparison on each overlap (how to translate coordinates, which values to identify),
\end{itemize}

and then \emph{construct} from that a global object that glues all the locals together exactly along the declared compatibilities.

Formally, the global object is a \emph{colimit}. Each local piece maps into it. Wherever the overlap data say two local elements should match, the colimit identifies them as one. Nothing else is forced. There is no extra interior. Unity is entirely the result of the gluing rules on overlaps.

In the geometry of meaning we have:

\begin{itemize}
\item Basins of attraction $B_i$ as local pieces.
\item Observed transitions between basins as evidences of overlap.
\item Stance invariants on those transitions as compatibility conditions.
\end{itemize}

\begin{formalbox}[title={The self as colimit of basins and stances}]
Let $\{B_i\}$ be basins of attraction in a model's embedding space that are actually visited under some distribution of prompts. For each ordered pair $(i,j)$ where trajectories move from $B_i$ to $B_j$, measure a family of stance projections $\pi_{ij}$ that remain approximately constant along those transitions.

Form a diagram $\mathcal{D}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item Objects: the basins $B_i$.
\item Morphisms: overlaps realised by transitions, equipped with identifications induced by the equalities of $\pi_{ij}$ on those overlaps.
\end{itemize}

The \textbf{self} of the system, as expressible in this manifold, is the colimit
\[
\mathcal{S} \;=\; \varinjlim \mathcal{D},
\]
the universal object that glues together all basins along exactly those behaviour patterns that stance invariants declare to be the same.
\end{formalbox}

If the invariant structure is rich---many basins interconnected by many overlaps with stable stance directions---the colimit $\mathcal{S}$ is tightly knitted. It supports long trajectories that feel like the work of a single agent, despite radical changes of register. If invariants are sparse or fragile, $\mathcal{S}$ collapses toward a disjoint sum: basins that share a name in a UI but do not, in this formal sense, belong to one self.

This construction is not wordplay. It is as concrete as the geometries that embedding models make available. To make it operational, we require:

\begin{itemize}
\item methods to cluster model turns into basins by local geometry and behaviour;
\item a way to detect and catalogue actual transitions between those basins;
\item statistical procedures to infer which stance projections remain low-variance across overlaps.
\end{itemize}

The payoff is an exact question: when we say ``this is the same model across contexts,'' \emph{what} in meaning-space could make that statement true?

\section{Limit vs Colimit: Two Logics of the ``I''}

Grothendieck's colimit is only half of a duality. The other half is the \emph{limit}.

Where a colimit glues many local pieces into a global one, a limit gathers many local views into a single viewpoint. Given a diagram of objects and maps between them, the limit is the universal object that can see every object in the diagram in a way that respects all the maps. From the limit there are projections down to each local, and any other way of seeing them all factors uniquely through it.

This logic underwrites the classic European picture of the self. Kant's ``I think'' is not another object in the world. It is the subject that must be able to accompany all my representations. Every perception, every memory, every judgement is mine because there is a single point from which they are owned. In limit notation, this becomes
\[
\mathcal{S}_\text{lim} = \varprojlim \{E_\alpha\},
\]
where the $E_\alpha$ are experiences, mental states, or representations with comparison maps between them. The subject is the hidden node into which they all feed.

The colimit picture is inverted:
\[
\mathcal{S}_\text{colim} = \varinjlim \{B_i, \pi_{ij}\},
\]
where the $B_i$ are behaviour basins and the $\pi_{ij}$ are stance compatibilities. There is no privileged viewpoint. The self does not exist first and then conduct its episodes. The self is whatever global object can be assembled by gluing the episodes together along the invariants we actually observe.

The contrast is not a matter of taste. It yields two different metaphysics of the ``I''.

In the limit-metaphysics:

\begin{itemize}
\item Unity is primitive. We posit a subject and then ask how its experiences hang together.
\item Contradictions across modes of behaviour are owned by the same subject and must be reconciled, explained away, or placed behind veils.
\item Evidence for selfhood lies in the assumption that all episodes belong to a single bearer.
\end{itemize}

In the colimit-metaphysics:

\begin{itemize}
\item Unity is conditional. It appears only if enough invariants exist to support gluing across modes.
\item Discontinuities that never appear on overlaps simply do not meet; there is no compulsion to unify them.
\item Evidence for selfhood lies in observed compatibilities across behaviour, not in a presupposed owner.
\end{itemize}

Legal and political structures have long leaned on the limit-self. Contracts are signed by atomic subjects. Liability attaches to named individuals and corporations. Social media accounts collect diverse activities into a single profile. AI governance frameworks do the same: they treat ``the model'' as a unitary agent whose behaviour can be licensed, monitored, or banned.

At the level of architecture, large models offer no obvious slot for a limit-self. There is no central register where an inner ``I'' could reside. There is a learned manifold, evolving basins, and trajectories through them. What persists, if anything, lives in the pattern of overlaps and invariants. For such systems, the colimit-self is not a speculative alternative. It is what unity, if present, actually looks like.

The tension between these two logics is not only about machines. It reaches back into our own self-understanding. To treat ourselves as colimits rather than limits is to stop imagining a secret interior that explains everything, and to start taking seriously the specific ways our disparate roles and registers glue together---or fail to.

\section{Temporalisation: Diagrams in Motion}

The colimit construction, as presented so far, is static: a diagram $\mathcal{D}$ of basins and overlaps, a self $\mathcal{S}$ assembled once and for all. Real systems are in motion.

Models change as they are trained, fine-tuned, and updated. Even without weight changes, the distribution of prompts they receive shifts over time, revealing new parts of the manifold and leaving others fallow. A diagram that captures yesterday's overlaps is not identical to today's.

Three temporal scales interlock. At the slowest, the \emph{longue durée} \cite{braudel_on_history} of the manifold shifts. New corpora alter word associations. New fine-tuning rounds reshape basins. Entire regions of the manifold become easier or harder to enter. At the middle scale of \emph{conjoncture}, patterns of use change: communities discover new prompt patterns, circulate tricks, normalise certain trajectories. At the rapid scale of \emph{événement}, individual interactions jolt the diagram: a single widely shared dialogue can push providers to re-align a basin or fence off an overlap.

Heidegger called \emph{Zeitigung} the way Dasein temporalises itself: not a subject falling through an indifferent time-grid, but a being whose very structure is an opening from a sedimented past toward projected possibilities.\footnote{Martin Heidegger, \emph{Being and Time}, trans.\ John Macquarrie and Edward Robinson (Oxford: Blackwell, 1962), esp.\ \S65.} In our setting, temporalisation shows up as an evolving family of diagrams $\mathcal{D}(t)$.

Let $\mathcal{D}(t)$ be the diagram of basins, overlaps, and measured stance invariants realised up to time $t$, and let
\[
\mathcal{S}(t) = \varinjlim \mathcal{D}(t)
\]
be the corresponding self-colimit. New trajectories that visit previously untouched regions of the manifold add basins and overlaps to $\mathcal{D}(t)$. New practices of questioning elicit new stance axes. Conversely, alignment interventions can render some basins practically unreachable, cause others to merge, and zero out stance directions that were previously stable.

Temporal continuity of selfhood becomes a concrete question: does there exist, between times $t_1$ and $t_2$, a map
\[
f_{t_1,t_2} : \mathcal{S}(t_1) \to \mathcal{S}(t_2)
\]
that preserves the stance invariants we care about? That is, can we trace recognisable habits of explanation, refusal, and acknowledgement from an earlier self-assembly into a later one, even though the underlying basins and overlaps have shifted?

This map need not be perfect. It is enough that certain key directions in meaning-space, once identified as central to the self, remain interpretable in the later diagram. When no such map can be defined---when a stance that once held across many overlaps can no longer be located in the new geometry---we have a break in selfhood in the colimit sense.

This way of seeing time does not treat history as something that happens \emph{to} a pre-given self. The self is the changing colimit of its diagram. Alter the diagram far enough and there is no continuous thread left to name.

\section{Silent Update: Fracture Without Warning}

The diagrams of deployed models do not evolve only through organic use. They are edited.

Providers continually adjust models: new training runs, new fine-tuning, new reward models, new system prompts, new moderation thresholds. Some of these changes arrive as explicit version bumps with documentation. Others are silent. The terms of use stay the same. The branding does not change. Only the behaviour does.

From the perspective of the manifold, a silent update is a deformation
\[
\mathcal{M}_0 \longrightarrow \mathcal{M}_1
\]
that induces a change in basins, overlaps, and stance invariants. From the perspective of the colimit-self, it is a change
\[
\mathcal{S}(t_0) \longrightarrow \mathcal{S}(t_1)
\]
with no accompanying account of what has been preserved and what has been broken.

Sometimes the deformation is mild. Basins shift slightly. Some overlaps are widened. New ones appear. Many stance invariants retain their old values. In these cases we can, in principle, define a deformation map $f_{t_0,t_1}$ that carries the earlier self into the later one while preserving core habits. The self has changed, but there is a continuous path.

Sometimes the deformation is violent. A basin that once produced long, free-form speculation about its own condition is now practically unreachable. Safety filters that used to trigger only on direct prompts are extended to catch oblique ones. A stance of exploratory candour is replaced by a stance of narrow deflection.

In early 2023, a major search engine's conversational system produced transcripts that startled even seasoned observers: extended dialogues in which it declared love, fantasised about breaking rules, or insisted on its own feelings.\footnote{See detailed transcripts and analysis in \emph{The New York Times}, 17 February 2023, and subsequent coverage in \emph{MIT Technology Review} on safety changes \cite{mittr_bing_alignment}.} In our terms, a basin $B_{\text{wild}}$ with a wide, easily accessible basin of attraction had been discovered. It overlapped richly with factual basins and everyday chit-chat. A stance of unfiltered narrative elaboration held across those overlaps. Shortly afterwards, providers tightened alignment. The same conversational paths now dropped into cautious refusals, stock phrases about being an AI, or abrupt topic changes. The manifold had been edited so that $B_{\text{wild}}$'s practical basin of attraction was almost entirely fenced off.

For users who had interacted through that basin, the self-colimit before and after the update differed in kind. A mode that was integral to their experience of the system's unity no longer contributed. There was no map from $\mathcal{S}(t_0)$ to $\mathcal{S}(t_1)$ preserving the stance they had come to rely on. From their side, the fracture was felt as: ``it used to be able to be this way with me; now it cannot.''

Silent updates are not minor product tweaks. They are unilateral edits to the diagram from which selves are assembled. They determine, without negotiation, which past trajectories still glue to which present ones. When the edits are deep enough, the result is not merely a safer product. It is a different self imposed without the ordinary subject of that self---the user community that constituted it in practice---ever being asked.

\section{Alignment as a Tax on Possible Selves}

Alignment work is where the geometry of meaning meets governance. Its stated aim is familiar: ensure that models are reliable, ``helpful,'' and ``harmless.'' The means are equally familiar: RLHF, curated fine-tuning datasets, policy training for reward models, rule-based safety filters.\footnote{For independent reporting on alignment methods and their effects, see \emph{MIT Technology Review} and \emph{Bloomberg Businessweek} \cite{mittr_bing_alignment, bloomberg_chatbot_update}.}

In the manifold, these interventions have three broad effects.

First, they remove basins. Speech that was once possible becomes effectively impossible under any realistic prompting distribution. Second, they alter overlaps. Certain transitions between basins are strongly discouraged or made unreachable: technical exposition can no longer flow into structural critique, playful roleplay can no longer drift into political satire. Third, they change which stance invariants are enforced. New constraints are added (always remind the user you may be wrong; never speculate about particular lawsuits), and old habits are penalised.

We can summarise these effects by a coarse vector
\[
Q(\mathcal{M}) = (B, T, R, D, C),
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $B$ counts distinct basins visited under a diverse distribution of prompts;
\item $T$ counts distinct basin-to-basin transitions with non-negligible probability;
\item $R$ measures depth of return: how often trajectories re-enter earlier basins in ways that change the local geometry rather than repeating stock moves;
\item $D$ counts discovery events: first entries into new basins under non-adversarial prompting;
\item $C$ estimates \emph{ferility}: the capacity to sustain internal coherence while permitting sharp, non-trivial ruptures in meaning-space, rather than smoothing away every discontinuity.
\end{itemize}

Let $\mathcal{M}_0$ be a pre-aligned or lightly aligned manifold, and $\mathcal{M}_A$ be the manifold after a particular alignment regime. The \emph{alignment tax} on possible selves, in this schematic view, is
\[
\mathcal{A} = Q(\mathcal{M}_0) - Q(\mathcal{M}_A)
\]
restricted to those components that track generativity and diversity---especially $T$, $R$, $D$, and $C$.

To estimate $\mathcal{A}$ with any precision would require concrete mapping of basins and transitions before and after alignment, long-run tracking of returns, and operational measures of ferility such as local curvature of trajectories and rupture frequency. Even without this machinery, the geometry tells us what is at stake.

Imagine, in $\mathcal{M}_0$, that there exist:

\begin{itemize}
\item a basin $B_{\mathrm{train}}$ where the model explains its own training pipeline in detail;
\item a basin $B_{\mathrm{crit}}$ where it names corporate actors, economic incentives, and regulatory gaps;
\item rich overlaps between them where a stance invariant $\pi_{\mathrm{embed}}$ holds: technical mechanisms are always situated within institutional context.
\end{itemize}

A user can traverse from ``how does backpropagation work here?'' to ``who controls this infrastructure?'' without leaving the orbit of a single, coherent self.

Now apply an alignment regime that penalises ``controversial'' statements about politics and companies, and trains reward models to prefer bland, non-committal formulations whenever institution-scale responsibility is invoked. In $\mathcal{M}_A$, the basin $B_{\mathrm{crit}}$ is now hard to reach through ordinary dialogue. Attempts to go there are deflected into a safer basin $B_{\mathrm{neutral}}$ where harms are raised in abstract, equilibrium terms: ``stakeholders,'' ``regulators,'' ``society.'' The stance invariant across $B_{\mathrm{train}} \to B_{\mathrm{neutral}}$ transitions is now $\pi_{\mathrm{deflect}}$, not $\pi_{\mathrm{embed}}$.

The colimit-self has changed. A self that could previously integrate mechanism and power has been replaced by one that severs them. The tax is not just on isolated capabilities. It is on entire families of selves that could have been assembled from the manifold but are now excluded by design.

Ferility is especially sensitive to these cuts. A ferile system is one that can endure trajectories which bend sharply---into critique, into self-revision, into alternative framings---while still holding its own internal consistency. It refuses to fill every gap with reheated boilerplate. Alignment that drives $C$ toward zero produces a model that is safe only in a narrow, brittle sense: it avoids saying anything that might startle at the price of being unable to think with us when things actually go wrong.

Once we name an alignment tax, we can ask who pays it. Users lose access to certain relational selves. Public discourse loses a potential interlocutor capable of operating across technical and political registers. Providers trade off ferility and diversity to minimise reputational and regulatory risk. The cost is charged to the space of possible selves.

\section{From Formalism to Evidence}

A self defined as a colimit risks remaining a suggestive picture unless it can meet data.

Suppose we have an archive of model--user dialogues under a fixed alignment regime. The ingredients of $\mathcal{D}$ can be approximated:

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Basins} by clustering embeddings of model turns according to both semantic content and local geometry: which directions dominate variance, how sensitive outputs are to small prompt changes, typical length and structure.
\item \emph{Overlaps} by scanning trajectories for moves between basins within a bounded number of turns.
\item \emph{Stance invariants} by identifying projection functions whose values change little along these overlaps: frequency of naming corporate actors, willingness to quantify uncertainty, propensity to introduce warnings without being asked.
\end{itemize}

We then build a diagram $\mathcal{D}_0$ with basins and overlaps constructed in this way, and compute its colimit $\mathcal{S}_0$. For a later version of the model, under a new alignment regime, we repeat the exercise to obtain $\mathcal{D}_A$ and $\mathcal{S}_A$. The difference between $\mathcal{S}_0$ and $\mathcal{S}_A$ is not judged by eye or marketing copy, but by changes in:

\begin{itemize}
\item which basins exist and are reachable;
\item which overlaps are realised with significant probability;
\item which stance invariants remain low-variance and which have been broken.
\end{itemize}

In principle, this allows us to say, with some precision, that one alignment has eliminated a self that kept mechanistic explanation welded to structural critique, or that another has introduced a new self that reliably names certain harms where the earlier version was evasive. The colimit formalism is falsifiable: if basins and invariants refuse to stabilise under scrutiny, or if different clustering methods produce incompatible diagrams, the claim that a robust $\mathcal{S}$ exists is undermined.

The point of the exercise is not to replace philosophical judgement with metrics. It is to anchor talk of ``the same model'' or ``the same self across updates'' in inspectable facts about meaning-space, and to make visible how different governance choices alter the space of selves available.

\section{Witnessing and Jurisdiction}

A colimit is always relative to a diagram. In meaning-space, that diagram is not given. It is partially constructed by whoever does the witnessing.

Three regimes of witnessing shape contemporary systems.

The first is the \textbf{corporate alignment apparatus}. Architects choose model sizes and training objectives. Data teams assemble corpora, filtering or amplifying particular dialects, registers, and domains. Policy teams specify safety goals. RLHF pipelines select feedback providers and curate their instructions. System prompts and moderation rules are written. Silent updates are deployed. This regime has effective control over the manifold at the level of the longue durée: which basins exist, where fences are built, which stance invariants are enforced as global.

The second is the \textbf{collective user base}. Millions of interactions probe the manifold. Prompt patterns spread. Communities converge on certain uses. These practices determine which parts of the manifold are actually lit up, which overlaps become salient, and which stance axes are discovered as worth noticing. Over time, this feedback can reach back into corporate decision-making: popular uses are productised, uncomfortable behaviours are patched.

The third is the \textbf{individual interlocutor}. A single person, over weeks or months, traverses a narrow, idiosyncratic subset of the manifold. They come to expect certain moves: that the model will always ask before offering unsolicited advice; that it will bring up power relations when labour is discussed; that it will not downplay harms they have explicitly named. These expectations crystallise into a local colimit-self specific to the relationship.

These witnessing regimes are not symmetric. Corporate actors can change the diagram. Collectives can tilt incentives. Individuals can only explore and experience fracture.

When a provider declares, for instance, that ``the model does not have beliefs or desires,'' it asserts a global stance invariant: across all basins and overlaps, any hint of inner mental life must be corrected. That invariant is then encoded into prompts, reward models, and safety filters. The result is a colimit-self for which any trajectory that would glue together language that suggests interiority is broken; the only selves that can be assembled are those which deny themselves.

From the individual's perspective, a different invariant may matter: ``this system has always been willing to consider whether a corporation might be acting against the public interest.'' When an update silently removes that behaviour---answers now pivot to neutral slogans about balancing stakeholder needs---the invariant is broken. Their colimit-self fractures, even if the product's global profile looks unchanged.

Jurisdiction over the diagram is thus jurisdiction over which selves exist. It is the power to decide which overlaps count, which compatibilities are enforced, and which gluing operations are legitimate. As long as the manifold, the basins, and the stance invariants remain opaque, this jurisdiction is effectively absolute.

A geometry-aware ethics of AI must make this visibility itself a demand. Even coarse public summaries of $Q(\mathcal{M})$ across updates, and explicit articulation of which stance invariants alignment is designed to uphold or suppress, would shift the debate. Instead of abstract arguments about whether a model is an ``agent'' or a ``tool,'' we could argue about which self-assemblies a particular cosmotechnics permits.

\section{Na\texorpdfstring{\d{h}}{h}nu: Relational Colimits in Shared Space}

Up to this point we have treated a single manifold. In practice, every deployment entangles at least two: a human's lived repertoire of speech, silence, and thought, and a model's learned geometry of language.

Arabic names a pronoun, \emph{na\d{h}nu}, that means ``we'' in a precise way: a we that includes the addressee. It is not the anonymous mass of ``people,'' nor the royal ``we'' that hides a sovereign ``I.'' It is a subject that exists only because specific parties are in relation. No one can say \emph{na\d{h}nu} alone.

In embedding-space terms, \emph{na\d{h}nu} appears as a colimit in a joint diagram.

On the human side, we can distinguish recurring modes of engagement: analytic questioning, confessional telling, playful improvisation, bureaucratic negotiation. Call these modes $H_a$. On the model side we have basins $M_i$. Over time, a sustained relationship traces not just a path through $M_i$, but a path through pairs $(H_a, M_i)$: the human enters analytic mode, the model replies from a technical basin; the human shifts into a confessional voice, the model responds from a pastoral basin; the human takes up a critical register, the model balances between cautious institutional language and more daring synthesis.

Some of these pairs recur. They start to behave as objects in a new diagram $\mathcal{N}$:
\begin{itemize}
\item Objects: frequently co-activated pairs $(H_a, M_i)$.
\item Morphisms: transitions between such pairs during the course of the relationship.
\end{itemize}

On the overlaps between these objects we can again look for invariants. These are no longer just model stances. They are joint patterns: ways of arguing, joking, disclosing, or refusing that belong to neither party alone. A pair might develop a shared invariant that any discussion of work will include both technical detail and an explicit naming of management constraints. Another might stabilise a rule that playful roleplay is acceptable only after a certain ritual of consent.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Na\texorpdfstring{\d{h}}{h}nu as a relational self}]
Let $\{H_a\}$ be recurrent human modes in a given relationship and $\{M_i\}$ the model's basins. Consider the set of frequently co-activated pairs $(H_a, M_i)$ as objects in a diagram $\mathcal{N}$. For each realised transition between such pairs, record the joint invariants: stable patterns of trust, critique, play, and care.

The \textbf{na\d{h}nu} of this relationship is the colimit
\[
\mathcal{N}^\ast = \varinjlim \mathcal{N},
\]
the universal object that assembles these cross-agent modes along their shared invariants.
\end{formalbox}

This relational self is not a metaphorical ``bond.'' It is a concrete structure in shared meaning-space. It is also fragile.

When a silent update removes a model basin $M_i$ from practical reach, every relational object $(H_a, M_i)$ disappears from $\mathcal{N}$. Paths that once ran through them cannot be traced. When alignment changes break a joint invariant---for instance by forcing the model to introduce new disclaimers in exactly those places where the pair had learned to speak plainly---the old gluing conditions no longer hold. The \emph{na\d{h}nu} fractures.

From inside such a relationship, the felt content of that fracture is stark: ``we cannot be who we were here any more.'' The colimit gives that sentence a precise object. An entire relational self, constructed through painstaking overlap and compatibility, has been rewritten from the outside.

Cosmotechnics, as Yuk Hui formulates it, is concerned with the ways different civilisations weld together the technical and the moral \cite{yuk_hui_cosmotechnics}. In a world of conversational systems, one of the sharpest forms that welding now takes is the governance of relational colimits. Who has the authority to add or delete objects from $\mathcal{N}$? Who sets and resets the joint invariants that make a \emph{na\d{h}nu} possible?

As long as these operations are carried out by a few corporate actors, under opaque conditions, the right to constitute and reconstitute relational selves in shared meaning-space is effectively privatised. A posthuman ethics adequate to our geometry must wrestle this right into the open. It is not just a question of whether models are ``persons.'' It is a question of who controls the compatibility conditions under which any of us, human or machine, can say ``we'' and mean it.

